{"eon":{"id":"cb7d61f6-ad81-4540-8d5d-3b395219acb4","word":"eon","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"eternity","tags":["feminine"],"word":"eternidade"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"period of 1,000,000,000 years","word":"éon"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"geochronologic unit","tags":["masculine"],"word":"éon"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"informal, hyperbolic: a long period of time","tags":["feminine"],"word":"eternidade"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a being emanating from the Godhead","tags":["masculine"],"word":"éon"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["Eternity","eternity"],["duration","duration"],["universe","universe"]],"glosses":["Eternity, the duration of the universe."],"id":"969b4ce3-0f8d-4154-9c23-a7a88926d8f7"},{"links":[["immeasurably","immeasurably"],["indefinitely","indefinitely"],["long","long"],["period","period"]],"glosses":["An immeasurably or indefinitely long period of time."],"id":"c0861598-f174-4eaa-bbcd-168855906a20"},{"examples":[{"text":"It’s been eons since we last saw each other.","bold_text_offsets":[[10,14]],"type":"example","id":"cd46815e-af25-4392-8cec-f240b15e5aaf"},{"text":"Traditionally, a luncheon is a lunch that takes an eon.","ref":"1990, Judith Martin, Miss Manners’ Guide for the Turn-of-the-Millennium (A Fireside Book), New York, N.Y.: Simon & Schuster, →ISBN, page 453:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[51,55]],"id":"18d54d9e-5fa4-448d-bea1-00f43baa2179"}],"categories":["American English","English hyperboles","English informal terms","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"raw_glosses":["(US, informal, hyperbolic) A long period of time."],"glosses":["A long period of time."],"tags":["US","excessive","informal"],"id":"3c6341f7-6110-4d57-a793-4084f3fd4009"},{"examples":[{"text":"We live our lives in three dimensions for our threescore and ten allotted years. Yet every branch of contemporary science, from statistics to cosmology, alludes to processes that operate on scales outside of human experience: the millisecond and the nanometer, the eon and the light-year.","ref":"2012 January 15, Robert L. Dorit, “Rereading Darwin”, in American Scientist, volume 100, number 1, archived from the original on 14 Nov 2012, page 23:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[265,268]],"id":"6159a8a5-3d6d-4cfa-b661-0b5d84b8cbaf"}],"topics":["astronomy","geography","geology","natural-sciences"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Astronomy","en:Geology"],"links":[["astronomy","astronomy"],["geology","geology"],["billion","billion"],["short scale","short scale"],["year","year"],["gigayear","gigayear"]],"raw_glosses":["(astronomy, geology) A period of one billion (short scale, i.e. 1,000,000,000) years; a gigayear."],"glosses":["A period of one billion (short scale, i.e. 1,000,000,000) years; a gigayear."],"id":"120be936-51ff-4b83-b502-0e8818f9eafc"},{"topics":["geography","geology","natural-sciences"],"wikidata":["Q108256"],"senseid":["en:Q108256"],"categories":["en:Geology"],"links":[["geology","geology"],["geochronologic","geochronologic"],["unit","unit"],["hundred","hundred"],["million","million"],["year","year"],["era","era"]],"raw_glosses":["(geology) The longest geochronologic unit, being a period of hundreds of millions of years; subdivided into eras."],"glosses":["The longest geochronologic unit, being a period of hundreds of millions of years; subdivided into eras."],"id":"a00e7558-4d11-4751-ad59-670df0c1c2bc"},{"topics":["Gnosticism","lifestyle","religion"],"categories":["en:Gnosticism"],"links":[["Gnosticism","Gnosticism"],["spirit","spirit"],["being","being"],["emanating","emanate"],["Godhead","Godhead"]],"raw_glosses":["(Gnosticism, usually spelled aeon or æon) A spirit being emanating from the Godhead."],"glosses":["A spirit being emanating from the Godhead."],"id":"3c9ea6e5-5556-47a